## Implementation constraints

- Initial scope is ABI v1 only: rn_verify_v1; no Bitcoin logic in Go. Deliver executable build.sh, Linux AArch64 service (DATADIR SOCKET_PATH), source/tests, HANDOFF.md and EXPERIMENTS.md when implementation is authorized. Baseline uses one job per admission/terminal WriteBatch; group commit belongs to optimization.
- Four concurrent native job slots; eight clients. Adapter batch: 64 transactions/16 MiB; transaction: 4 MiB/4,096 combined items; JSON text: 40 MiB. Outstanding work: both 1,024 jobs and 64 MiB raw payload, including finished results awaiting commit.
- Admission atomically persists complete payload, digest, sequence, metadata/accounting and id index before accepted. Digest is SHA-256 of ordered mode byte, operation byte, little-endian uint64 length, raw bytes. Retry identity ignores priority.
- Terminal commits strictly follow admission sequence. Atomically write receipt, delete pending/cancel, release accounting, advance checkpoint; preserve id index. Cancellation intent is durable before ok. The terminal boundary is native WriteBatch call_entry, not socket arrival or an earlier Go state change. Storage failure must stop without acknowledging an uncommitted transition.
- Version 1 schema and zero-padded 20-digit sequence keys are prescribed. Recovery must preserve cancellations, receipts, payload and accounting. Missing scalar counters mean zero only on a fresh database. Native RocksDB open supplies exclusive writer locking. WAL enabled and sync=true for all transitions; retain prescribed storage settings.
- Per-client responses remain ordered; admission response precedes its event. Terminal events are best-effort after durability; disconnect never cancels. Drain accepted work before a zero-exit shutdown. Slow clients must not block workers or durable progress.
- Arena inputs remain immutable and alive until every native worker returns, including cancelled jobs. V1 item pointers must point into rn_arena_data. Arena allocation failure during evaluate returns execution_failure; after acknowledgment, stop and preserve pending for restart.

## Candidate design and validation focus

A serialized state/storage coordinator with four workers is a plausible baseline. Retain results until their admission sequence can terminalize; serialize cancellation and terminal-write decisions. Keep socket writes outside storage coordination and gate events behind admission responses. Validate recovery invariants, retry/conflict, ordered completion, cancellation races, worker ownership, backpressure, disconnects and draining. No scheduler/barrier hooks in the service.

reuse/storage.go already supplies native open/get/WriteBatch/sync settings and ownership cleanup; it supplies no scheduling, recovery or service policy. Link cgo with /adapter include/library/rpath plus -lrocksdb.

